FINAL ANSWER:
**Short answer:** Troy Goode holds the most code that survives today. Douglas Christopher Wilson is second on lines, but he has the most commits and hasn't committed since 2021.

All figures below come from `surviving-lines` at HEAD (62628c0). I re-ran it and got the same numbers as the pre-computed brief.

**Who holds the surviving code (non-blank lines, of 1,662; counting blank lines, of 1,985)**

| Author | Lines | Share | With blanks | Commits | Last commit |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Troy Goode `<<email redacted>>` | 605 | 36.4% | 762 | 93 | 2019-05-27 |
| Douglas Christopher Wilson | 551 | 33.2% | 629 | 143 | 2021-09-07 |
| Íñigo Marquínez Prado | 80 | 4.8% | 95 | 2 | 2024-05-14 |
| Jon Church | 59 | 3.5% | 72 | 3 | 2026-01-16 |
| Sebastian Beltran | 57 | 3.4% | 67 | 2 | 2025-07-23 |

- **Troy Goode may be two people's worth of lines under two identities.** A second address, `troy@laptop.example`, holds 44 lines (2.6%) and last committed on 2026-04-10. If it's the same person, Troy holds 649 lines (39.0%). This is a proposal only. I haven't applied a `.mailmap` merge, and only you can confirm it's one person.
- **Bus factor is 2 by lines.** Troy and Douglas together hold 69.6% of the code. Five authors are needed to reach 80%.
- **Active bus factor is 1.** Within the 365 days before HEAD, 9 of 44 authors committed. They hold 860 lines (51.7%), and Troy alone is the active majority. The other 48.3% belongs to people with no commit in that window.

**Where the risk sits**

- **Orphaned files:** 6 of 18 files (7.8% of lines) have an inactive top owner and no active person with 20%+. The largest are `.github/workflows/scorecard.yml` (61 lines, Íñigo Marquínez Prado), `HISTORY.md` (41 lines, Douglas), and `LICENSE` (18 lines, Douglas).
- **Single-owner files:** `scorecard.yml` (Íñigo, 61 lines), `.github/workflows/codeql.yml` (Sebastian Beltran, 53 lines), and `test/vary.js` (Troy, 32 lines).
- **By directory:** `test/` is 820 lines (49.3%), mostly Troy at 47.3%. Root files are 323 lines (19.4%), mostly Douglas at 29.4%. `.github/` is 305 lines (18.4%), mostly Douglas at 30.2%. `lib/` is 214 lines (12.9%), mostly Troy at 61.7%.

**Caveats**

- **The reformat commit changes Douglas's number.** HEAD commit 62628c0 ("style: reformat js…") rewrote 1,016 lines. `surviving-lines` sets it aside by default and credits those lines back to earlier authors. Without that correction, Douglas drops to 376 lines (22.6%) and Troy rises to 510 (30.7%). Whether Douglas's share is 33% or 23% depends on this call.
- **The activity window is anchored to HEAD, not today.** The "active" window ends at HEAD's commit date, 2026-04-12, not today (2026-10-24). I computed this from the commit table, not from `surviving-lines`: measured from today, Sebastian Beltran's last commit (2025-07-23) falls outside the 365-day window, which would make the active bus factor and the 48.3% figure shift slightly. I did not re-run the tool with a different window.
- **Surviving lines measure survivorship, not quality or understanding.** They don't capture review work, design done outside the code, or code deleted on purpose.
